<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 40 "Feel Flow" M3e ACs 4-7 - THE PUPPET.

  WHY THIS EXISTS. Section 3.4 asked for a "per-body-part display"; M3e gives
  it a body - a dockable stick figure whose joints are driven, BY POSITION,
  from the pendulum ensemble's own exported state. The one thing that makes
  it worth having rather than merely pretty is that NOTHING in it is
  synthesized: a part's displacement is sqrt(unitPower) * cosPhi, both read
  from the decoded aspects at the frame's hop, with no oscillator and no
  nominal frequency anywhere in the paint path. A puppet animated from a
  tempo would move beautifully and mean nothing.

  WHAT THIS CASE GATES.
  (1) THE THREE OUTCOMES ARE DISTINCT, which is the whole shape of the
      feature and the only thing a script can actually separate:
        - no analysis yet          -> valid=0, everything 0
        - mid-material             -> valid=1, JOINTLY nonzero (minAbsSum)
        - past the material's end  -> valid=1, everything ~0 (maxAbsSum)
        - after a staling edit     -> valid=0 again
      Note the second and third differ ONLY in the numbers: "valid but
      still" is a real answer (the material ended; the puppet stands still),
      deliberately not the same answer as "no analysis".
  (2) THE STALE RULE IS THE PAINT PATH'S, not the pose function's. The verb
      mirrors SMainWindow::updateFeelFlowPuppet_ exactly - a stale track
      yields the INVALID pose whatever is still cached - so the gate and the
      screen cannot disagree about it. That is what the last phase asserts.
  (3) DETERMINISM: the same frame asserted twice with the same full
      describe line. The pose is a pure function of an immutable snapshot,
      so anything else would mean something is being recomputed.
  (4) The widget PAINTS (a PNG grab mid-material). Coverage, not an oracle -
      aesthetics are explicitly not gated (M3e AC 7).

  RE-PINNED AGAIN 2026-08-28 by proposal 44 C1a, which moved the pose off the
  DRIVE-LOCKED phase arg(z) and onto the METRICAL phase (groove.dyn v3's
  cosMet). Every component moved, because every part had been oscillating at
  roughly the tatum regardless of its own metrical level - measured up to 16x
  its seed. They now land on their levels: bounceY 2.00 Hz against a 2.0 seed,
  sway 0.50 against 0.5, armSwing 0.99 against 1.0, hipShift 0.25 against 0.25.

      was:  bounceY=0.4274 sway=0.2289 armSwing=-0.0561 headNod=-0.2210 hipShift=0.1432
      now:  bounceY=0.4317 sway=-0.2301 armSwing=0.3214 headNod=0.0983  hipShift=0.0003

  energySum is UNCHANGED at 1.3862 - C1a moved the phase and not the envelope,
  which is exactly the claim that |z| was never the problem. hipShift at 0.0003
  is not a dead unit: twobar is a 0.25 Hz oscillation and frame 240000 happens
  to sit near its zero crossing, where before the drive-locked phase would have
  put it anywhere.

  The head's own relative offset is now SMALL (0.0983) and that is correct
  physics rather than a regression: the trunk oscillates at 0.5 Hz, well below
  the neck's 1.264 Hz corner, so a head with this much inertia simply goes with
  it. Measured in WORLD space the head moves at 0.58 Hz with ratio 0.21 -
  carried, not flung.

  RE-PINNED 2026-08-28 by proposal 44 C1, which stopped driving the head from
  the "reference" unit and DERIVES it from the trunk instead (a one-pole lag,
  option D). Only the head moved; the other four components are bit-identical.

      was:  headNod=0.9262  energySum=2.0951   (head <- "reference", the tatum gauge)
      now:  headNod=-0.2210 energySum=1.3862   (head <- trunk, lagging)

  The sign flip is the point: the head is now displaced OPPOSITE to the trunk's
  current lean, which is what lagging behind it means in the trunk's own frame.
  energySum fell because the head borrows the TRUNK's energy rather than the
  retired reference unit's - it is exactly as "participating" as the trunk it
  hangs on. absSum is 1.0766, still comfortably over the pinned minAbsSum=0.50.

  MEASURED (first run, 2026-08-24, this box), a_offset15.wav, the default
  5-unit ensemble, hopFrames=480 (1622 hops = 16.22 s of material):

    frame 240000 (5.00 s, mid-material):
      pose: valid=1 bounceY=0.4274 sway=0.2289 armSwing=-0.0561
            headNod=0.9262 hipShift=0.1432 energySum=2.0951
      -> absSum = 1.7818.  PINNED minAbsSum="0.50", a floor with ~3.5x
         margin: the quantity is a sum of five oscillating components, so
         it legitimately dips as the ensemble passes through a
         near-quadrature moment, and pinning it near the measurement would
         be pinning a PHASE rather than "the figure is moving". Note the
         five components carry BOTH signs (armSwing is negative here) and
         four of the five are well away from zero, which is what makes
         "jointly nonzero" a fair reading of one summed bound.
    frame 900000 (18.75 s, past the 16.22 s of material):
      pose: valid=1 bounceY=0.0000 sway=0.0000 armSwing=0.0000
            headNod=0.0000 hipShift=0.0000 energySum=0.0000
      -> absSum = 0 EXACTLY (the past-material branch returns before any
         component is written), so maxAbsSum="0.0001" is a tolerance
         against nothing rather than a measurement margin.

  The determinism phase pins the FULL mid-material line above, all five
  components at 4 decimals - the strongest per-frame claim available, and
  the one that fails loudest if anything in the read path stops being pure.
  Verified identical on the COLD run (empty store) and on three consecutive
  WARM runs, which matters here for a reason worth recording:

  A REAL DEFECT THIS CASE FOUND ON ITS SECOND RUN. The default-ensemble
  fallback for unit NAMES (M3b, added because physUnitNames_ is in-memory
  job state that stays empty when the first bounce of a process hits a warm
  store) was applied to a LOCAL used only for the metric ids. The pose maps
  units onto body parts BY NAME out of the snapshot's own unitNames, which
  stayed EMPTY - so every warm run produced valid=1 with all five
  components at exactly 0, while every metric row read fine. The puppet
  simply stood still on the second run of any case, and nothing before this
  one read unitNames closely enough to notice. Fixed by resolving the
  fallback into SFeelFlowUiData::unitNames itself, beside nUnits, so the
  metric ids and the pose are built from ONE list.

  Not RUN_SERIAL: no smaragd.ini key is read or written (the puppet owns no
  preference at all; the dock's placement rides the existing ui/windowState
  blob, which a test-case run never writes because it never shows the
  window).
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="feel_flow_puppet">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <sidecar-root/>

    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="0" filePath="../groove/a_offset15.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- ============ (1a) BEFORE any analysis: the invalid pose ========== -->
    <!-- No bounce has ever completed, so the track is stale by definition
         (STrack::feelFlowStale()'s own "never bounced" answer) AND there is
         no snapshot to read. Either alone would produce valid=0; the point
         is that the puppet shows the neutral figure with its note rather
         than a body driven by nothing. -->
    <assert-feel-flow-pose trackPath="0" frame="240000" valid="0"
                           maxAbsSum="0.0001"/>

    <feel-flow-analyze target="track" trackPath="0"/>
    <wait-analysis timeoutMs="60000"/>

    <!-- The v2 phase channels are what the pose reads; without groove.dyn
         (or with a pre-M3e v1 store, which decodes to an EMPTY dyn) every
         pose below would be INVALID. Asserting the aspect here is what
         makes the valid=1 assertions statements about the POSE rather than
         about whether the analysis ran. -->
    <assert-groove-aspect aspect="groove.dyn" trackPath="0" stale="0"
                          expectExists="true" minRecords="1500" maxRecords="1650"/>

    <!-- ============ (1b) MID-MATERIAL: valid and genuinely moving ======= -->
    <!-- 240000 frames = 5.00 s at 48 kHz, comfortably inside the 16.22 s of
         material. minAbsSum is the "the figure is MOVING" claim - a floor
         over the SUM of all five |components|, because any single joint can
         legitimately sit near zero at a given phase while the body is in
         motion. See the header for the measurement and the margin. -->
    <assert-feel-flow-pose trackPath="0" frame="240000" valid="1"
                           minAbsSum="0.50"
                           grabPng="feel_flow_puppet_mid.png"/>

    <!-- ============ (1c) PAST the material: valid, but STILL ============ -->
    <!-- 900000 frames = 18.75 s, past the last analyzed hop. This must NOT
         be the invalid pose (there IS an analysis) and must NOT hold the
         last pose (which would read as "still grooving" over silence). -->
    <assert-feel-flow-pose trackPath="0" frame="900000" valid="1"
                           maxAbsSum="0.0001"/>

    <!-- ================== (3) determinism ============================== -->
    <!-- The same frame, twice, pinned to the full line: a pure function of
         an immutable snapshot has exactly one answer. -->
    <assert-feel-flow-pose trackPath="0" frame="240000" valid="1"
                           contains="pose: valid=1 bounceY=0.4317 sway=-0.2301 armSwing=0.3214 headNod=0.0983 hipShift=0.0003 energySum=1.3862"/>
    <assert-feel-flow-pose trackPath="0" frame="240000" valid="1"
                           contains="pose: valid=1 bounceY=0.4317 sway=-0.2301 armSwing=0.3214 headNod=0.0983 hipShift=0.0003 energySum=1.3862"/>

    <!-- ============ (2) a staling edit: back to the invalid pose ======== -->
    <!-- The gain moves the track root's content epoch (proposal 37 P3a put
         twGainStage between the plugin chain and the rewire precisely so a
         volume change is observed on every path), so the bounce no longer
         describes this chain. The cached snapshot is STILL THERE and still
         decodable - which is exactly why this bites: only the painter's own
         stale rule, mirrored by the verb, turns it into an invalid pose. -->
    <set-track-volume trackPath="0" volume="-6.0"/>
    <assert-feel-flow-pose trackPath="0" frame="240000" valid="0"
                           maxAbsSum="0.0001"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
